{
  "image_name": "1fe8f25a40f902a052c0ab6c48fb28cb.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two related DNA repair pathways. On the left, it shows nucleotide excision repair of a bulky DNA lesion on a duplex. Damage is first recognized by XPC and XPE, then local DNA unwinding is carried out by TFIIH helicase components such as XPD and XPB, with XPA, RPA, and RAD23B helping organize the repair complex. Endonucleases, including XPF–ERCC1 and XPG, cut on both sides of the lesion, excising a short damaged fragment. DNA polymerase then fills the gap and ligase seals the nick, restoring the strand.\n\nThe right side focuses on repair of an interstrand crosslink at a replication fork. The lesion is detected at the fork, where the Fanconi anemia pathway assembles, including the FA core complex, FANCM, FANCD2, FANCI, and BLM. These factors recruit nucleases to make dual incisions that “unhook” the crosslink from one strand. The remaining damage is then bypassed by translesion synthesis and resolved through a combination of repair synthesis, nucleotide excision repair, and homologous recombination involving FANCI and BLM. Overall, the figure highlights coordinated lesion recognition, incision, synthesis, and ligation steps, with the crosslink pathway appearing more complex because it must also restart stalled replication.",
